.TH newscache.conf 5 "Jun 2004"
.SH NAME
newscache.conf \- configuration file for newscache(8)


.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/newscache.conf


.SH DESCRIPTION
The file
.I @SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.conf
defines all the 
.BR "NewsCache 's"
configuration options. It is also used for
.BR updatenews(8)
,which is responsible for submitting articles from the 
outgoing spool directory and prefetching newsgroups
and
.BR newscacheclean(8),
which cleans up the Cache.

.TP
NewsCache is a free cache server for USENET News. NewsCache acts to news reading clients like a news server, except that it stores only those articles that have been requested by at least one client. NewsCache targets problems of the current News System like network bandwidth consumption or the IO load caused by news clients. NewsCache acts to the upstream news server like a news reader programm.

.TP
It supports principles the following modes (for a detailed description read ServerSpec Configuration Options below):
.TP
.I "cached"
NewsCache store every retrieved article in his internal cache. If this article is retrieved by another news reader client, then NewsCache can get the article from his cache instead of retrieving this article again from the upstream newsserver.
.TP
.I "not-cached"
NewsCache act as a "proxy" and does'nt store retrieved article in his local cache. This is usefull for combined multiple local newsserver configuration.
.TP
.I "offline"
NewsCache does'nt connect to the upstream newsserver. Only the specified newsgroups are retrieved by
.BR updatenews(8)
and stored in the cache. This is usefull in dialup configurations. The news reader clients can only access this newsgroups.
.TP
.I "semi-offline, cached"
The overview informations are loaded with
.BR updatenews(8)
and the articles retrieved by NewsCache, if a news reader client request it. The article is stored in the internal cache.


.SS General Configuration Options
Numeric values are plain decimal numbers. A value that is no number, or
is out of range, is a configuration error that names the file and line.
The ranges: ClientTimeout 0 to 86400 seconds; MaxConnections 1 to
1000000; NiceServer, NiceClient and NiceClean \-20 to 20; the two
Timeouts 0 to 2147483647. The options of a Server entry have their
ranges below, under ServerSpec Configuration Options.
.TP
.I "ConfigVersion version"
Specifies the syntax version of the configuration file. The only
version this NewsCache accepts is 5, the version this manual page
describes; any other number is a configuration error, so that a file
written for an older NewsCache (versions 1 to 4) is refused instead of
being misread. The line may be left out. If specified, it should be
specified as the first configuration option.
.TP
.I "ServerType type"
This may be either
.I standalone
for a standalone server or
.I inetd
if NewsCache 
is started from inetd. A standalone server has the advantage that the
configuration file will be read once only, for the cost of an extra
process running all the time. Usually, you use a standalone server, if
NewsCache is frequently accessed. The default value is standalone.
.TP
.I "PidFile filename"
On startup NewsCache will write its PID to the given file. This can
then be used by system scripts to gracefully stop NewsCache by sending
a SIGTERM to the parent process. No default value: without PidFile,
no pid file is written. NewsCache writes the file before it switches
to
.IR Username ,
and removes it when it stops (SIGTERM, SIGINT); for that, the
directory must be writable for
.IR Username ,
for example /run/newscache owned by news. Where it isn't, as in /run,
the file is only emptied. Only a regular file is written: a symbolic
link, a hard link or anything else at that name is left alone (with a
warning in the log), so that the user of a writable directory can't make
root overwrite another file. That user can still put another pid in the
file; where scripts send signals by it, a directory only root can write
is safer. Under systemd no pid file is needed.
.TP
.I "Username username"
Change the user id to username as soon as NewsCache has bound to
.IR CachePort .
No default value: started as root without Username, NewsCache and
every client session keep running as root, and the log says so with a
warning. Set it (for example to news).
.TP
.I "Groupname groupname"
Change the group id to groupname as soon as NewsCache has bound to
.IR CachePort .
No default value.
.TP
.I "Admin email-address"
The email address of this NewsCache's administrator.
.TP
.I "Hostname name"
The host name NewsCache puts into the Message-IDs it makes for posted
articles that have none. The default is the fully qualified domain name
of the system, as
.B newscache \-f
prints it (localhost if there is none); set it if that is wrong.
.TP
.I "AccessList { AccessSpec AccessSpec ... }"
Client access configuration. The first AccessSpec that matches the client
is used, in the order of the file, so put narrower entries before broader
ones and the Default entry last; an entry after Default is never used.
.TP
.I "NewsServerList { ServerSpec ServerSpec ... }"
Defines a list of news servers and the newsgroups to retrieve from which
news server. For a detailed description of
.I ServerSpec 
see the ServerSpec Configuration Options section below.
.TP
.I "Timeouts active-db group-description"
Timeouts in seconds for the retrieval of the active database (first parameter) and the description of the newsgroups (second parameter). If a client requests "list active", then newscache checks if the active database timeout is reached and first requests the active informations from the upstream servers before returning the data to the client. The second parameter is read but has no effect: NewsCache doesn't fetch the newsgroup descriptions; LIST NEWSGROUPS answers from the file .newsgroups in the spool directory, which you create yourself (lines "group description"). Note: if the ServerSpec section contains the Options offline or semioffline, then the data will be not downloaded from the upstream server. You need
.BR updatenews(8) .
The default values are 3600 seconds for both parameter.
.TP
.I "ClientTimeout t"
Terminate the connection after t seconds inactivity. The default value is 1200 seconds. The same limit applies to each write to the client and, plus 10 seconds, to each read inside a command (the article of a POST): a client that stops reading its replies, or stops sending in the middle of an article, is disconnected too.
.TP
.I "AuthFailureDelay seconds"
After a failed login (AUTHINFO PASS), wait this many seconds before answering 481, multiplied by the number of failed logins in the connection (15, 30 and 45 with the default; after the third failure the connection is closed). This slows down password guessing. The default is 15 seconds; 0 answers at once, the maximum is 3600.
.TP
.I "History on|off"
With on, keep a history of the articles in the spool (the directory .history in SpoolDirectory) and serve ARTICLE, HEAD, BODY, STAT and the message-ID forms of XHDR, XPAT and HDR from the spool when it has the article; see the manual, "Articles by Message-ID". The history counts toward SpoolSize; newscacheclean removes it when it alone takes more than half of SpoolSize, and when History is off. The default is off: every message-ID request goes to the news server.
.TP
.I "MaxArticleSize bytes"
The largest article NewsCache takes from a news server. A larger one is an error: NewsCache closes the connection to that server (the rest of the article is unread on it), the client gets 403, nothing is stored, and the log says so. The default is 1048576 (1 MiB), the range 1024 to 1073741824. A session holds an article in memory as a whole. Lines from a news server outside articles (status lines, overview records, the active list, LISTGROUP) are limited to 16384 bytes each, with the same effect; lines in articles count only towards MaxArticleSize.
.TP
.I "MaxPostSize bytes"
The largest article a client may post (counted with CRLF line ends). A larger one is read to its final "." and refused with 441; the session goes on. The default is 1048576 (1 MiB), the range 1024 to 1073741824.
.TP
.I "MaxConnections number"
Maximal number of simultanous connections. This Parameter works only in standalone mode. The default number is 32.
.TP
.I "SpoolDirectory directory"
The directory, where the cached newsgroups and articles should be stored. The default value is /var/cache/newscache.
.TP
.I "SpoolSize KBytes"
The maximum size of the spool area, a plain number of KBytes. This is controlled by the
.BR newscacheclean (8) .
The default value is 1048576 KBytes (1 GByte).
.TP
.I "LogDirectory directory"
Directory, where all the log-files should go. No default value. Only
used when NewsCache is built with \-\-without\-syslog. The programs open
their log file (newscache.log, newscache_newscacheclean.log,
newscache_updatenews.log) before they switch to
.IR Username ,
so a directory only root can write is enough when they start as root.
Only a regular file with one link is written: a symbolic link, a hard
link or anything else at that name is refused with an error on stderr,
so that a user who can write the directory can't make root write to
another file. The one exception is a symbolic link to the program's own
stderr (such as /dev/stderr), which the container image uses.
.TP
.I "LogStyle [strict-inn][hostname][ip-address]
StrictINN style logging; log client's hostname and or IP-address.
.TP
.I "CachePort port"
Listen on port, on all addresses, for incoming requests. Port may be a
service name (usually listed in /etc/services) or a port number, optionally
preceded by a #-sign. Ignored if ListenTo is set; "ListenTo DEFAULT:port"
does the same and is the better choice. This directive is ineffective if
NewsCache is started from inetd.
.TP
.I "PrefetchFile filename"
Specifies the file that lists the newsgroups updatenews prefetches, one
per line. A line may also be a wildmat pattern
.RI ( de.* ", " comp.lang.[cg]o )
that selects the matching groups of the active database, or
.B !
followed by a pattern or group name, which leaves the matching groups out.
Empty lines and lines starting with # are skipped. See
.BR updatenews (8).
No default value.
.TP
.I "NiceServer n"
Increment priority of the server process by n. See also
.BR nice(1) .
The default value is 0. In this version, the session processes (one per
client) use NiceServer too: a session forked by the standalone server
adds it once more to the priority it inherits.
.TP
.I "NiceClient n"
Meant as the increment for the client (session) processes. The default
is 10. In this version it is read but has no effect; the sessions use
NiceServer.
.TP
.I "NiceClean n"
Increment priority of the newscacheclean process by n. See also
.BR nice(1) .
The newscacheclean process is a housekeeping process. See also
.BR newscacheclean(8) .
The default value is 3.
.TP
.I "ListenTo specification"
The Address, where NewsCache is listening, if running in
standalone mode.  
In a multihomed environment the address of one NIC can be 
specified. The default Port is nntp from /etc/services. For
the port-number an entry fom /etc/services or a port-number
can be specified. 
.B DEFAULT
(or
.BR DEFAULT:port )
listens on all IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, with one IPv6 socket
that accepts IPv4 clients too; on a host without IPv6, on all
IPv4 addresses. Use 0.0.0.0 to listen on IPv4 only.
.SS AccessSpec Configuration Options
.IP
.nf
\fIClient\fR [fqdn-or-part | ip-address/netmask]
{
	\fIallow\fR read post debug none authentication
	\fIList\fR \fINGSpecification\fR
	\fIRead\fR NGSpecification
	\fIPostTo\fR NGSpecification
	\fIAuthentication\fR authenticationSpecification
}

.fi
.IP "Client ip-address/netmask"
The netmask is a prefix length (0 to 32 for IPv4, 0 to 128 for IPv6)
or, for IPv4, a mask such as 255.255.255.0 whose bits are contiguous.
A plain address without a netmask means that host only. An empty prefix
("10.0.0.0/"), one out of range and a mask with gaps are configuration
errors.
.IP "allow [read] [post] [debug] [none] [authentication]"
Allow read, post, debug for this Client. The default is none: a client whose entry allows nothing gets "502 You have no permission to talk" and is disconnected.
.I authentication
lets such a client stay connected so that it can log in. It gives no right by itself and doesn't make a login required: whether a login is possible, whether it is required, and what it adds is set by Authentication (see there). If only authenticated users are allowed for this network, specify authentication alone together with a login that adds the rights; see example "Only authenticated users".
Before the login, such a client gets 480 for the commands that need a right it doesn't have yet; CAPABILITIES, MODE READER, AUTHINFO, HELP and QUIT are answered in any case.
Where no login can add a right (the entry has Authentication none, or the client has logged in already), a command that needs it gets "502 access denied", and POST from a client without the post right gets "440 posting not permitted" (RFC 3977). An unknown command gets "500 What?".
The greeting and MODE READER answer 200 only if the client may post at that moment, and 201 (posting prohibited) otherwise, also before a login that would add the post right (RFC 3977, RFC 4643).
CAPABILITIES lists only what the client may do at that moment: before a required login (Authentication unix without fields) or on an entry that allows only the login, it lists AUTHINFO USER but not READER, HDR, OVER, LIST or POST; after the login it lists the rights the login gave.
.IP "List \fINGSpecification\fR"
Permits client access to the specified newsgroups.
.IP "Read \fINGSpecification\fR"
Allow reading specified newsgroups. Same format as List.
.IP "PostTo \fINGSpecification\fR"
Allow posting to specified newsgroups. Same format as List.
A post to another group is refused with 441 after the article has been sent (RFC 3977 allows only 240 or 441 there); while a login could add the group, the text says "authentication required".
.IP "NGSpecification"
is a comma seperated list of newsgroups, where the last part of 
newsgroup may be substituted by * standing for any string.  Each 
newsgroup is retrieved from the news server with the longest match.
Examples: * (all newsgroups), at.* (the whole at. hierarchy), *,!alt.binaries.* (all newsgroups without alt.binaries hierarchy).
.IP Authentication
Type of authentication plus authenication specific options.
Only
.B unix
without fields makes the login required: until then every command but CAPABILITIES, MODE READER, AUTHINFO, HELP and QUIT gets 480, whatever the entry allows.
With file:, pam (also without fields), pam+file and unix with fields the login is optional: the client has the rights of its entry from the start, and a login adds those of the user. An entry that allows only authentication still lets nobody in without a login, but for another reason: it gives no rights, so the commands that need one get 480 until the login (those that need none, such as DATE, work).
A failed login (wrong password, unknown user, a stored hash that can't be checked) is answered with 481, AUTHINFO PASS without AUTHINFO USER with 482, and AUTHINFO after a successful login, or where the entry has Authentication none, with 502 (RFC 4643). Possible values are:
.IP none
no further authentication necessary
.IP file:/path/to/file
formatdescription:
.nf
		username:password:list:read:postto:allow
.fi
Only username and password are required. Fields missing at the end add nothing, like empty ones: a line \fBalice:hash\fR only checks the login, and the client keeps the rights of its Client entry.
The additional access specification are added to the normal List Read and PostTo Parameters (see above and the example section). The password field is a encrypted password (md5crypt of crypt) or plaintext (configured with --enable-plainpass). htpasswd from the apache project can create such a password. In the allow field can be additional permission specified (Example: authenticated user can be use the post command).
.IP unix:list:read:postto:allow
Use unix authentication (getpwnam or getspnam). The additional access specification are added to the normal List Read and PostTo Parameters and allow(see above and the example section). If only unix is specified, then all clients must step throuh the authentication.  If further fields specified, then unauthenticated clients have the access specified with the parameter allow, List, ReadTo, PostTo and authenticated clients uses the arguments and modify the original parameters.
.br
It is also possible to specifiy empty fields, example: unix:::*:debug. Which means, that the PostTo parameter is extended with *. What actual settings are used by NewsCache can be determined with the xdebug dump authorization command.
.br
NewsCache checks the password after it has switched to \fIUsername\fR (usually news), and only root may read /etc/shadow. So unix authentication can't check local accounts with shadow passwords, unless NewsCache runs as root, which is not recommended. NewsCache then logs "cannot read the shadow password". Use file: instead, or pam with a PAM module that works without root.

.IP pam:list:read:postto:allow
Use pam authentication. For additional description of the fields see:
.I unix:list:read:postto:allow
The PAM Servicename can be specified with the configuration parameter
.I PAMServicename
.br
pam_unix, the usual module for local accounts, can't check them either: run as a user other than root, its helper unix_chkpwd only checks that user's own password. NewsCache logs PAM's error together with this hint. Modules that work without root, such as pam_sss (SSSD), pam_ldap or pam_pwdfile, are fine.

.IP pam+file:/path/to/file
The authentication is handled over pam and the additional permission values are taken from the file. The recordformats in the file are valid:
.nf
#this is a comment
hugo:at.test:at.test:at.test:post
@mygroup:de.*:de.*:de.*:post
*:us.*:us.*::
.fi
The fields are List, Read, PostTo and the allow flags, as in the file: format but without the password. After PAM has accepted the password, the rights of every line that applies are added to those of the entry: the line *, the lines @group of the groups the user is a member of (supplementary groups, as listed in the group database), and the user's own line, in that order. In the example, hugo can list, read and post to at.test and use the post command; if hugo is a member of mygroup, de.* is added; and every user, hugo included, can list and read us.*. A user without a line of their own gets the rights of * and of their groups. If no line applies, the login is refused.

.IP "PAMServicename name-of-service"
This parameter specifiy the name of the PAM Service. The default is newscache. You can use different PAM settings for each Client network. For further explanation of PAM see the System administrators PAM Guide. This Guide can be downloaded from http://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/libs/pam/pre/doc/

.SS ServerSpec Configuration Options
.nf

\fI[Server | NoServer]\fR news.host Service 
{
	\fIRead\fR GroupSpec
	\fIPostTo\fR GroupSpec
	\fIGroupTimeout\fR seconds
	\fILimitGroupSize\fR number
	\fIBindFrom\fR address
	\fIRetries\fR nr-of-retries
	\fIConnectTimeout\fR seconds
	\fIConnectBackoff\fR seconds
	\fIReadTimeout\fR seconds
	\fIUser\fR username
	\fIPassword\fR password
	\fITLSCAFile\fR file
	\fIOptions\fR flags
	\fICommands\fR commands
}
.fi
.IP
to specify what newsgroups have to be retrived from which news server
and which articles have to be submitted to which news server or
.I ServerSpec
may be:
.IP "NoServer { Read \fIGroupSpec\fR PostTo \fIGroupSpec\fR }"
to specify what newsgroup has to be retrieved by no news server and
to which newsgroups no articles should be posted to. Or:
.IP "Server news.host Service
.IP
.I news.host
is the name of the news server and
.I Service
indicates the port, where the news server is listening for connections.
.IP "Read \fINewsgroupList\fR"
A comma separated list of wildmat patterns: the groups NewsCache takes from this server. NewsCache fetches the active list with one LIST ACTIVE command per pattern; for example, Read at.*,de.* sends "list active at.*" and "list active de.*" to the server (RFC 3977 7.6.3). This can save bandwidth. If the server refuses LIST ACTIVE with a pattern, NewsCache asks it for the whole list once instead and keeps the groups that match. No default value.
.IP "PostTo \fINGSpecification\fR"
NewsCache post an article to the best matching upstream newsserver. If the article cannot be submitted immediately, it is kept for later transmission. See the example: Multiple Upstream NewsServer.
.IP "GroupTimeout \fIseconds\fR"
specified how long the group information (for instance retrieved with the command GROUP at.linux) is valid (in seconds). Note: if the ServerSpec section contains the Options offline, then the data will be not downloaded from the upstream server. You need
.BR updatenews(8) .
The default value is 600 seconds, the range 0 to 2147483647. The overview records of a part of a group that a client reads again are compared with the upstream server again after this time too; NewsCache fetches them in steps of 10000 article numbers, only for the numbers clients ask for.
.IP "LimitGroupSize \fInumber\fR"
Clients see only the newest \fInumber\fR article numbers of each group
of this server: a larger range is cut at its start. The default is 0, no
limit; the range is 0 to 4294967295. Large groups don't need it.
.IP "User \fIusername\fR"
.IP "Password \fIpassword\fR"
The login (AUTHINFO USER and PASS) for this server, if it needs one.
NewsCache logs in right after it has connected (after STARTTLS with
starttls), and again when the server asks for it with 480 in the middle
of a session.
.B newscache \-p
and xdebug don't show the password; they print the comment
.I "# Password set, not shown"
instead.
.IP "TLSCAFile \fIfile\fR"
A PEM file with the CA certificates to verify this server's TLS certificate against, for servers with a private CA. Without it, NewsCache uses the system's trust store.
.IP "BindFrom \fIaddress\fR"
Local address used for connections to this upstream server. It must be a numeric IPv4 or IPv6 address; host names are not resolved. No default value.
.IP "Retries \fInumber\fR"
How often NewsCache connects to this server again when the connection
breaks during a command (the server closes it, answers 400, or
ReadTimeout runs out), and sends the command again. If that fails
too, the client gets an error. The default is 3, the range 0 to 100; 0
gives up at once. A 503 counts only when the server has closed the
connection right behind it (old INN sent "503 Timeout" on an idle
connection); otherwise it is an ordinary error reply. A first
connection that fails isn't retried.
.IP "ConnectTimeout \fIseconds\fR"
Give up a connection attempt to this server after this many seconds.
The limit applies to each address of the server's name in turn (an IPv6
and an IPv4 address are tried one after the other) and covers only the
TCP connection; the greeting and everything after it fall under
ReadTimeout. The default is 0, which leaves it to the system: that can
take minutes for a server that doesn't answer. The range is 0 to 86400.
.BR updatenews (8)
uses it too.
.IP "ConnectBackoff \fIseconds\fR"
After a connection to this server has failed (refused, timed out, or
failed while it was set up: greeting, STARTTLS, login), don't try again
for this many seconds: a command that needs the server fails at once
instead. Each session (one per client) and each updatenews run keeps
its own record. The default is 0, which tries every time; the range is
0 to 86400.
.IP "ReadTimeout \fIseconds\fR"
Give up the connection to this server when it sends (or accepts) nothing for this long: each read and write, not a whole reply, so a large article on a slow line is fine. NewsCache connects again (up to Retries times) or answers the client with 403. The default is 300, the range 0 to 86400; 0 waits for ever.
.IP "Options \fIflags\fR"
Specifies a list of options that should be turned on or off. The default flags are: cached setpostflag. Valid flags are:
.nf
	\fI[not-]setpostflag\fR
	\fI[not-]cached\fR
	\fI[not-]offline\fR
	\fIsemioffline\fR
	\fIssl\fR
	\fIstarttls\fR
	\fInoverify\fR
	\fIdontgenmsgid\fR
.fi
.RS
.TP
.IP "[not-]setpostflag"
set postflag to n in the active database, if posting to the news server is not allowed. not-setpostflag, setpostflag indicates whether the postflag of newsgroups on servers without posting permission should be set to `n'.
.IP "[not-]cached"
not-cached, cached indicate, whether groups from this server should be cached or not.
.br
.IP "[not-]offline"
NewsCache does'nt connect to the upstream newsserver. Only the specified newsgroups are retrieved by
.BR updatenews(8)
and stored in the cache. This is usefull in dialup configurations. The news reader clients can only access this newsgroups.
.IP "semioffline"
The overview informations are loaded with
.BR updatenews(8)
and the articles retrieved by NewsCache, if a news reader client request it. The article is stored in the internal cache.
.IP "ssl"
Use TLS from the start of the connection, usually on port 563 (nntps).
.IP "starttls"
Connect in plain text and switch to TLS with the STARTTLS command, usually on port 119. If the server refuses STARTTLS or the TLS handshake fails, NewsCache gives up the connection; it never continues in plain text.
.IP "noverify"
With ssl or starttls: don't verify the server's certificate. The connection is encrypted, but anyone in the middle can impersonate the server. NewsCache logs a warning for every such connection. Only for servers with a self-signed certificate; prefer TLSCAFile.
.IP "dontgenmsgid"
NewsCache gives a posted article without a Message-ID header one of its
own (see Hostname). With dontgenmsgid, an article that goes only to
this server is passed on without one, and the server makes it. An
article that goes to several servers always gets one, so that it isn't
posted twice under different IDs.
.RE
.IP
With ssl or starttls, NewsCache verifies the server's certificate during the TLS handshake: it must chain to a trusted CA, be valid now and match the host name (or IP address) in the Server line, which is also sent as SNI. Otherwise the connection fails and the error is logged.
.IP "Commands \fIcommands\fR"
All commands are switched on by default and NewsCache tries itself to find out which of the commands are supported. In some situations it might be necessary to deactivate a command manually (eg. it is not implemented correctly by your news server). To switch off, prepend the command with not-support commands: listgroup, over
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP "Reducing Load from a NewsServer - cached mode"
.nf

ConfigVersion 5
ListenTo DEFAULT
AccessList {
	Client 127.0.0.1/255.255.255.255 {
		allow read post
		# No xdebug command for unauthenticated
		# clients.
		List *
		Read *
		PostTo *
		Authentication unix::::debug
		# xdebug command for authenticated clients.
		# the empty fields (:::) doesn't modify
		# List, Read, PostTo parameters.
	}
	Default {
		allow none
	}
}
NewsServerList {
	Server news.host nntp {
		Read at.*
		PostTo at.*
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
}

.fi

.IP "Local NewsGroups"
Configuration to transparently include local Newsgroups. The local Newsgroups are not cached.
.nf

ConfigVersion 5
ListenTo DEFAULT
AccessList {
	Client 127.0.0.1/255.255.255.255 {
		allow read post debug
		List *
		Read *
		PostTo *
		Authentication none
	}
	Default {
		allow none
	}
}
NewsServerList {
	Server upstream.newsserver nntp {
		Read *
		PostTo *
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
	Server local.newsserver #120 {
		Read *
		PostTo *
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options not-cached
	}
}

.fi

.IP "Offline Mode"
Offline Mode for ppp dialup connections. The Newsgroup of interesst have to list in prefetch-file and retrieved with the updatenews program.
.nf

ConfigVersion 5
ListenTo DEFAULT
AccessList {
	Client 127.0.0.1/255.255.255.255 {
		allow read post debug
		List *
		Read *
		PostTo *
		Authentication none
	}
	Default {
		allow none
	}
}
NewsServerList {
	Server upstream.newsserver nntp {
		Read *
		PostTo *
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options offline
	}
}

.fi

.IP "Only Authenticated Users"
Only authenticated users are allowed. This example show how to setup.
.nf

ConfigVersion 5
ListenTo DEFAULT
AccessList {
	Client 127.0.0.1/255.255.255.255 {
		allow authentication
		List !*
		Read !*
		PostTo !*
		Authentication pam:*:*:*:read,post
	}
	Default {
		allow none
	}
}
NewsServerList {
	Server upstream.newsserver nntp {
		Read *
		PostTo *
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
}

.fi

.IP "Multiple Upstream NewsServer"
We are connected to the main upstream NewsServer f.q.d.n and various other NewsServer. The new Newsgroups after modifications of the NewsServerList are available after the Timeout ActiveDB Value. This can be forced with updatenews -a (see manual page updatenews).
.nf

ConfigVersion 5
ListenTo DEFAULT
AccessList {
	Client 127.0.0.1/255.255.255.255 {
		allow read post
		List *
		Read *
		PostTo *
		Authentication none
	}
	Default {
		allow none
	}
}
NewsServerList {
	Server f.q.d.n nntp {
		Read *
		PostTo *
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
	Server news.redhat.com nntp {
		Retries 3
		Read gated.*
		PostTo gated.*
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
	Server nntp.perl.org nntp {
		Retries 3
		Read perl.*
		PostTo perl.*
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
	Server news.php.net nntp {
		Retries 3
		Read php.*
		PostTo php.*
		GroupTimeout 1200
		Options cached
	}
}

.fi

.SH THE xdebug COMMAND
.IP "telnet localhost 119 and the xdebug command"
Note: the client's entry must have debug in its allow line. The following example shows a typical xdebug session, for the entry
.nf

	Client 127.0.0.1 {
		allow debug
		List at.*
		Read at.*
		PostTo at.*
		Authentication file:@SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.auth:*:*:*:post,read
	}

.fi
where the auth file has a line for myusername with the fields *:*:*:post,read. After the login, the rights of that line are added: the patterns * and the flags read and post.
.nf

telnet localhost 119
Trying 127.0.0.1...
Connected to localhost.
Escape character is '^]'.
201 NewsCache @NC_VERSION@, accepting NNRP commands, posting prohibited
xdebug dump authorization
100 authorization data follows
127.0.0.1/32{
  access_flags=4 ( debug)
  list=at.*
  read=at.*
  postto=at.*
  PAMServicename newscache
  authentication=file:@SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.auth:*:*:*:post,read

}
\&.
authinfo user myusername
381 password please
authinfo pass mypassword
281 OK.
xdebug dump authorization
100 authorization data follows
127.0.0.1/32{
  access_flags=7 ( read post debug)
  list=at.*,*
  read=at.*,*
  postto=at.*,*
  PAMServicename newscache
  authentication=file:@SYSCONFDIR@/newscache.auth:*:*:*:post,read

}
\&.
quit
205 Good bye
Connection closed by foreign host.
.fi 

